   ['Intrinsic motivation comes from interest and enjoyment in the task; extrinsic motivation comes from outside rewards or pressures such as marks, prizes or fear of punishment. Abraham Maslow\'s hierarchy of needs (1943) places physiological needs, then safety, belonging, esteem and self-actualisation; a child who is hungry or afraid cannot focus on learning, which is why school feeding and safe schools matter. Albert Bandura\'s self-efficacy (belief in one\'s ability to succeed) and Carol Dweck\'s growth mindset (belief that ability can grow with effort) also affect effort and persistence.',
    'Howard Gardner\'s theory of multiple intelligences (1983) proposed several distinct intelligences, such as linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal and naturalist. Research support for it is limited, and "learning styles" matching has little evidence, but varied activities help engage different learners.',
    'Inclusive education means that learners with disabilities and other special needs learn in ordinary schools with the support they need, as promoted by the Salamanca Statement (1994) and the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (2006). Teachers differentiate by adapting content, process, product and environment: seating a learner with low vision at the front, giving extra time, using large print, sign language or tactile materials.'],
